<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Bund</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bund is the waterfront promenade along the west bank of the Huangpu River in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>. Laid out from 1845 as the river frontage of the British settlement, it became lined with the banks, trading houses, hotels and consulates of the foreign community, many in grand neoclassical and Art Deco buildings of the 1920s and 1930s. It remains the city's best-known landmark.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-bund.jpg" alt="The Bund" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 钉钉 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Bund_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The term bund was borrowed into English from Hindustani, where it meant a dyke or embankment; in the Chinese treaty ports it was applied to an embanked quay. The Chinese name means outer bank, distinguishing this riverfront from the inner bank next to the old walled city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>. The Bund stretches one mile (1.6 km) along the Huangpu River, traditionally from Yan'an Road in the south to the Waibaidu Bridge over Suzhou Creek in the north. It faces the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skyscraper">skyscrapers</a> of Lujiazui in Pudong and centres on a stretch of Zhongshan Road, named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area lies north of the old walled city. It began as a British settlement, and the British and American settlements were combined in the International Settlement in 1863. From the 1860s to the 1930s it was the centre of the foreign establishment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, a legally protected treaty port, with banks and trading houses from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and other countries, the British consulate, the Shanghai Club and a newspaper. By the 1940s it housed the headquarters of many major financial institutions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p>After the Communist victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-civil-war">Chinese Civil War</a> many institutions moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> in the 1950s, and statues of colonial figures were removed. From the late 1970s buildings returned to their former uses. After typhoon floods a tall levee was built, and in the 1990s Zhongshan Road was widened to ten lanes, which removed most of the parkland. From 2008 traffic was reorganised, with six lanes put into a tunnel and the promenade widened; the Bund reopened on 28 March 2010, before the World Expo. On 31 December 2014 a stampede in a crowd estimated at 300,000 killed at least 36 people and injured 47.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Some 52 buildings in Western classical and modern styles, among them Neo-Classical, Gothic Revival and Art Deco, line the west side of the road. They include the Hongkong and Shanghai Banking Corporation's building of 1923, the Customs House of 1927 with a clock modelled on Big Ben, and Sassoon House with the Cathay Hotel, now part of the Peace Hotel. At the northern end, Huangpu Park holds the Shanghai People's Heroes Memorial Tower, built in 1993.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Bund” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Shanghai was opened to foreign trade by the Treaty of Nanking, a British settlement was laid out along the <b>Bund</b> in 1845 and an American one at Hongkou in 1848.</blockquote><a href="/state/shanghai-international-settlement">Shanghai International Settlement</a></li></ul></section>
